,"text":"    @TARGET: x86;"},{"highlight_end":42,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    @MACRO_NAME: is_x86_feature_detected;"},{"highlight_end":18,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    @MACRO_ATTRS:"},{"highlight_end":75,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// A macro to test at *runtime* whether a CPU feature is available on"},{"highlight_end":30,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// x86/x86-64 platforms."},{"highlight_end":8,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    ///"},{"highlight_end":82,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// This macro is provided in the standard library and will detect at runtime"},{"highlight_end":84,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// whether the specified CPU feature is detected. This does **not** resolve at"},{"highlight_end":84,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// compile time unless the specified feature is already enabled for the entire"},{"highlight_end":85,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// crate. Runtime detection currently relies mostly on the `cpuid` instruction."},{"highlight_end":8,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    ///"},{"highlight_end":84,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// This macro only takes one argument which is a string literal of the feature"},{"highlight_end":84,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// being tested for. The feature names supported are the lowercase versions of"},{"highlight_end":66,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// the ones defined by Intel in [their documentation][docs]."},{"highlight_end":8,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    ///"},{"highlight_end":31,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// ## Supported arguments"},{"highlight_end":8,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    ///"},{"highlight_end":85,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// This macro supports the same names that `#[target_feature]` supports. Unlike"},{"highlight_end":82,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// `#[target_feature]`, however, this macro does not support names separated"},{"highlight_end":81,"highlight_start":1,"text":"    /// with a comma. Instead testing for multiple features mus